          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "Guidance describes four drug classes as first-line for most adults with newly diagnosed high blood pressure who have no other condition that calls for a different drug. The source is the hypertension first-line treatment guidance (major cardiology society consensus summary):\n\n- Thiazide-type diuretics\n- Angiotensin-converting-enzyme (ACE) inhibitors\n- Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs)\n- Dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers\n\nThe same guidance says an ACE inhibitor and an ARB should not be combined, because the combination adds harm without adding benefit.\n\n**When drug treatment starts:** The guidance says drug treatment is added to lifestyle measures when there is established heart or blood-vessel disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10% or more. For stage 1 hypertension without these features, it advises lifestyle measures first and reassessment after three to six months. The passage describing when treatment usually starts with two drugs was cut off, so I cannot confirm which group it applies to.\n\n**Where authorities differ:** The 130/80 mm Hg diagnostic threshold is a United States position. European guidance starts hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and treats 130–139 / 80–89 as high-normal rather than stage 1. Because of this, many people United States guidance calls stage 1 would be advised lifestyle measures alone under European guidance. Any threshold quoted should say which convention it uses.\n\n**Strength of evidence:** The retrieved material does not say how strong the trial evidence is for these drug classes or how confident each recommendation is. I could not find a grading of the evidence, so I cannot say how firmly the evidence supports this list.\n\nA clinician can explain which group a person falls into and which drug class suits their situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
